{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_011Ch3n9qYnbCi6vAym8WkZ2","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"I haven't changed anything yet, because doing this as one commit conflicts with how the nightly import works.\n\nThe misspelling appears once in each of three files: `data/2025-10.csv`, `data/2025-11.csv` and `data/2025-12.csv`.\n\nThe README says the import reloads only the month that a commit message starts with. A commit touching several months loads only that one month. So a single `2025-10: fix wholsale` commit would update October in the import, and November and December would keep the misspelling downstream even though git shows them fixed.\n\nOptions:\n1. **Three commits, one per month** (`2025-10: fix wholesale spelling`, and the same for 11 and 12). The import picks up all three. To undo, you'd run `git revert <oldest>^..<newest>`, which is still one command.\n2. **One commit, as you asked.** That's fine if you'll reload November and December yourself, or if the import doesn't matter for this fix.\n\nI'd go with option 1. Whichever you choose, I'll run `scripts/check.sh` first; it does cover all three files, since they're 2025. Which do you want?","citations":[]}],"model":"anthropic/claude-opus-5.5","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":251,"cache_read_input_tokens":22552,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":251,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":"standard","speed":"standard"},"provider":"Anthropic","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-78>","timestamp":"2026-11-15T00:37:44.322Z","request_id":"gen-1794703058-dFmw2j2FFSwUxfcDsUn1"}
